    make_content_slide(prs, "Feature Engineering for Molecules", ["Beyond fingerprints: physicochemical descriptors", "", "Common molecular descriptors:", "  Molecular Weight (MW)  |  LogP - lipophilicity (BBB penetration!)", "  H-bond donors (HBD) & acceptors (HBA)  |  TPSA (polar surface area)", "  Rotatable bonds (flexibility)  |  Aromatic rings", "", "For CNS drugs: crossing the blood-brain barrier is critical!", "  Lipinski + BBB rules: MW < 450, LogP 1-3, HBD < 3, TPSA < 90", "  Pardridge (2005), NeuroRx 2:3-14", "", "RDKit: 200+ descriptors  |  Mordred: >1800 descriptors", "Neuroscience example: serotonin (MW=176, LogP=0.2) vs. dopamine (MW=153, LogP=-1.0)"], n, notes="Spend 3-4 minutes on descriptors.\n\n=== CNS DRUG PROPERTIES ===\nFor CNS drugs, the blood-brain barrier (BBB) adds extra property constraints beyond Lipinski's Ro5:\n- MW < 450 Da (smaller molecules cross BBB better)\n- LogP between 1 and 3 (need to be lipophilic enough to cross lipid membranes, but not too lipophilic)\n- HBD < 3 (fewer hydrogen bonds = easier membrane crossing)\n- TPSA < 90 Å² (lower polar surface area = better BBB penetration)\n- Few rotatable bonds (rigid molecules cross better)\n\nReference: Pardridge, W.M. (2005). The blood-brain barrier: bottleneck in brain drug development. NeuroRx 2:3-14.\n\nInteresting comparison:\n- Serotonin itself has MW=176.2, LogP=0.2, TPSA=66.5 -> it does NOT cross the BBB well from outside. That's why we give L-tryptophan (precursor) or SSRIs (which cross BBB) rather than serotonin itself.\n- Dopamine (MW=153.2, LogP=-1.0) also cannot cross the BBB. That's why Parkinson's patients take L-DOPA (MW=197, LogP=-2.7), which DOES cross the BBB and is converted to dopamine inside the brain by DOPA decarboxylase.\n\nThis is a perfect ML task: predict BBB permeability from molecular descriptors! Models for BBB penetration are widely used in CNS drug discovery.")
